# NO MODULE OR SOLUTION SECRET IS DECLARED HERE (#1056). MODULE_IDENTITY_SECRETS,
# MODULE_REGISTRATION_SECRETS and SOLUTION_REGISTRATION_SECRETS are deleted with
# everything that read them: a module proves which module it is with its pod's
# projected ServiceAccount token (audience `accounts`), which accounts reviews
# and holds against the build the principal's delivered authority approves.
# Execution binding is wired with the declared-presence surface below, so a host
# with no coordinate mints for no module.

# Declared solution presence (issue #952): the coordinate this host answers for.
#
# It is the ONE declaration that turns the surface on. Empty leaves the whole of
# it off — no reconciler and no delivery endpoint — which is the default while
# the runtimes still register themselves: nothing on this host is declared, and
# every solution is present because it heartbeats.
#
# There is NO mount setting. It was SOLUTION_HOST_BINDINGS_DIR, a projected
# ConfigMap volume, and both it and the directory reader are deleted. A document
# reaches this host exactly one way: delivery POSTs a signed carrier to
# `/platform/_delivery/{presence,authority}`, the host verifies it on receipt and
# persists it, and the reconciler reads its desired set from that durable inbox.
# A mount could not be the desired set — it is the CURRENT one, so a document
# that arrived, was recorded as desired, failed to apply and then disappeared was
# never retried, and the mount dropped the carrier so a restore could not
# re-verify what it had accepted.
#
# Declaring the coordinate where no TokenReview can be performed refuses to
# boot: a delivered carrier is authorised by TokenReview, so a host that cannot
# reach its API server has no input at all.
#
# The coordinate is the exact string an operator declared on the environment the
# renderer read (`environments[].host.coordinate`). It is never derived here: a
# coordinate this host invented would match nothing delivery ever wrote, so every
# document would be refused as targeting a different host.
SOLUTION_HOST_COORDINATE=

# The ownership domains this host accepts delivery from, comma-separated.
# Required with the coordinate, and empty refuses to boot.
#
# Core refuses a document whose ownership domain this host has not stated, and
# the reason is that the applied record cannot bound a binding's FIRST
# generation: there is nothing yet to compare a domain against, so without the
# declaration any delivery could claim an unseen binding ID under a domain of
# its own choosing and own it from then on. It is the host's to declare because
# Core cannot know which delivery is entitled to a name it has never seen.
SOLUTION_HOST_OWNERSHIP_DOMAINS=

# WHERE the trust anchor is read from is NOT a setting, and there is deliberately
# no key here for it. It is the constant `infra.SolutionHostTrustAnchorPath`, and
# the two documents on it are `trusted_root.json` (the mirrored Fulcio/Rekor
# root) and `verification_policy.json` (the identity allowlist, and which
# ownership domains each accepted signer may deliver under).
#
# This WAS a key, SOLUTION_HOST_TRUST_MOUNT, and that was a hole rather than a
# convenience. Workspace environment is delivered by the COMPOSITION, so a
# composition could point the verifier at a policy and a root it wrote itself,
# sign its own presence documents with a key that policy listed, and pass every
# downstream check — because every check was against an anchor it chose. That is
# not a check that can be hardened: the thing under attack is where the check's
# own inputs come from, so it has to hold by construction. A deployment needing a
# different path changes the pod spec that mounts it, which the platform renders
# and the composition cannot supply.
#
# The anchor's ABSENCE refuses the boot, naming the anchor. A host configured to
# verify and unable to must not start and claim it is verifying: refusing per
# document instead makes "this host has no trust root" and "delivery is shipping
# something bad" the same observable.

# How a delivered carrier's bundle is checked. The only value is `keyless`.
# Required with the coordinate, with NO DEFAULT, because a host must say how it
# checks a carrier rather than inherit one.
#
# `keyless` verifies a Sigstore bundle against the mirrored trust root and the
# identity allowlist on the trust anchor, never a key. Verification is
# OFFLINE: the bundle carries its own transparency evidence, and the delivered
# namespaces are default-deny egress, so an online Rekor or Fulcio lookup would
# HANG rather than fail fast.
#
# There used to be a second value, `local`, which performed NO cryptography and
# attested every carrier as the identity `local`, gated on this host's coordinate
# beginning `local/`. It is DELETED. The gate was a string comparison against an
# operator-declared value, so a deployed host configured with a local coordinate
# admitted any file any mount writer dropped — and because the coordinate is
# self-asserted on both sides, the misconfiguration was not self-defeating.
#
# A local cluster runs the SAME verifier at the same strength: the public-good
# trusted root, and a verification policy that declares itself local and lists
# the developer as a `person` signer, on the anchor. The local declaration is in
# that platform-owned document, never in this file, and it is BOUND TO ONE
# CLUSTER by the SHA-256 of the cluster's certificate authority: at boot the host
# requires its projected CA bundle to be exactly that certificate and the API
# server under it to accept the pod's own token, keeps that pinned transport for
# every later cluster call, or refuses to start. So a
# deployed host refuses a locally signed document through the ordinary allowlist
# check, and refuses a local profile at boot — not through a mode flag, and not
# through anything this environment can say. SOLUTION_REGISTRATION.md §8 ("The
# local profile's trust anchor") is the profile.
#
# A configuration still carrying `local` fails to boot by name rather than
# falling through to `keyless`.
SOLUTION_HOST_TRUST_POLICY=

# How often the host re-reads the delivery inbox. Delivery POSTs a carrier at its
# own cadence and the reconciler is not woken by it, so the pass is a poll and
# this is the convergence bound. Empty uses the built-in default (30s).
SOLUTION_HOST_BINDING_INTERVAL=
